    "body": "### Problem\n\nHaving `u` / `<C-R>` as mappings that do exactly the same as non-mapped LHS makes undo/redo behave inconsistently when multicursor is active.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Create the following `init.lua`:\n\n    ```lua\n    -- The real world use case has RHS like `u<Cmd>lua ...<CR>` where `...` is some internal bookkeeping\n    vim.keymap.set('n', 'u', 'u')\n    vim.keymap.set('n', '<C-r>', '<C-r>')\n\n    -- For easier repro\n    vim.keymap.set('n', 'gm', 'yiwp')\n    vim.api.nvim_buf_set_lines(0, 0, -1, false, { 'aa', 'bb' })\n    vim.bo.modified = false\n    ```\n\n2. `nvim --clean -u init.lua`\n3. `vip` - `Q` to initialize multicursor.\n4. `gm` - `gm` to perform two undoable edits.\n5. `u`. This undoes both of edits and moves extra cursors out of place.\n6. `:restart!` - `:unmap u`\n7.  `vip` - `Q` - `gm` - `gm`. Now `u` - `u` works as expected, but pressing `<C-r>` afterwards performs incorrect redo with extra cursors out place.\n\nNote: everything works as expected if multicursor is enabled via `vip` - `Q` - `q=` (i.e. disabled follow mode).\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nUndo/redo should work as mappings with multicursor regardless of whether follow mode is active.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-dev-1693+g0f7538c4ab\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nNo\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nEndeavourOS Linux x86_64, 7.2.6-arch2-1\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nGhostty 1.3.1-arch2\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-ghostty\n\n### Installation\n\nAppimage",
